<h1>Security Model</h1>
<p class="subtitle">Gate handles credential-shaped data and modifies files. Its controls are scoped; review the exact operation before relying on them.</p>

<h2>Local and network behavior</h2>
<p>Ordinary scans run on the local machine and Gate has no intentional product telemetry. Opt-in credential verification sends the matched credential value needed for the check directly to the named provider API. Update operations can also contact remote sources. Provider, proxy, DNS, and network infrastructure may observe request metadata.</p>

<h2>Files and permissions</h2>
<p>Gate attempts to create sensitive local artifacts with restrictive owner permissions on supported Unix-like systems. Permission semantics vary by operating system, filesystem, mount, umask, and backup/sync tooling; inspect the resulting files in your environment.</p>

<h2>Writes and rollback</h2>
<p>Core source-file rewrites use a same-directory temporary file and rename, reducing partial-write risk for that path. Not every config, environment, vault, cache, report, snapshot, or state write currently uses the same atomic pattern. A crash-safe claim therefore applies only to the code path that implements it.</p>

<h2>Subprocesses</h2>
<p>Some Gate paths use <code>execFileSync</code> with argument arrays. Other current paths use shell-backed synchronous commands. Do not rely on a global “no shell” guarantee. Review untrusted repository paths and any generated command before execution.</p>

<h2>Output and artifacts</h2>
<p>Supported terminal and report paths redact detected credential values. That is a mitigation, not a proof that a secret can never appear through an error, debug path, provider response, generated artifact, or future format. Review output before sharing it. Generated purge/recovery artifacts can contain sensitive values or destructive commands and must be protected and reviewed.</p>

<h2>Snapshots and vault state</h2>
<p>Batch-fix snapshots can contain the pre-fix source and environment files in plaintext under <code>~/.gate/snapshots</code>. Vault encryption uses a local AES-256-GCM key stored alongside local ciphertext state; compromise or loss of that key affects every stored value. Short <code>VAULT:&lt;id&gt;</code> references created by interactive remediation have no public CLI or application-runtime resolver.</p>

<h2>Credential verification</h2>
<p>Current adapters are intended to call validation or identity-style endpoints without mutating user resources. APIs and provider behavior can change, and some checks use POST for non-mutating queries. Review the documented endpoint and scopes before opting in. A “live” result shows that the provider accepted the credential for that check; “inactive” can also reflect scope, endpoint, account, network, or provider changes.</p>

<h2>Rules and signatures</h2>
<p>Rule-pack signatures can detect modification relative to the trusted signing key. They do not make rule logic correct and do not protect against compromise of the key, updater, runtime, or trust configuration.</p>

<h2>Audit history</h2>
<p>Gate's local audit data is hash-chained, which can make some edits detectable when the chain is verified. It is tamper-evident, not append-only or tamper-proof: a user or attacker with sufficient filesystem access can replace, truncate, or recompute local state.</p>
<div class="warning-box"><strong>Known plaintext audit exposure:</strong> the current writer masks <code>match</code> but retains the separate scanner <code>secret</code> field when it spreads a finding into <code>~/.gate/audit.jsonl</code>. <code>gate audit --export</code> can therefore disclose raw credentials. Treat the audit file and exports as secret-bearing until the writer is corrected.</div>

<h2>Generated hooks</h2>
<p>Generated hooks export <code>GATE_HOOK_TYPE</code>, but the CLI branches that suppress config verification and re-stage fixes check <code>GATE_PRE_COMMIT</code>. Keep <code>verify: false</code> for hook-driven scans, and inspect the Git index after remediation. Hooks also have explicit bypass variables and cannot replace protected-branch or server-side controls.</p>

<h2>Offline mode</h2>
<p><code>GATE_OFFLINE=1</code> is intended to disable Gate's documented verification and update network features. Enforce air-gapped policy at the operating-system/network layer as well; an environment flag is not an external network boundary.</p>

<h2>Known limits</h2>
<ul>
  <li>Pattern and entropy detection can miss secrets and flag benign strings.</li>
  <li>Auto-fix is pattern-based and can produce an incorrect or incomplete rewrite.</li>
  <li>File history, generated reports, caches, and backups may preserve material after a source edit.</li>
  <li>Credential verification transmits the credential to a provider and cannot guarantee the provider performs no side effects.</li>
  <li>Verification labelled inactive currently lowers severity to low, which can move a finding below a CI threshold.</li>
  <li>Auto-fix can write a <code>NAME_NEW</code> value while leaving source pointed at <code>NAME</code> when an environment variable conflicts.</li>
  <li>Gate has not had an external security or efficacy audit.</li>
</ul>

<div class="warning-box"><strong>Practical policy:</strong> inspect diffs, rotate exposed credentials at the provider, test the application, and keep an independent secret-scanning control in CI. Gate output is evidence, not proof that a repository is clean.</div>
